// core/no_prelude — sentinel import to opt out of the
// auto-injected magic prelude.
//
// `import "core/no_prelude";` in a program signals the checker
// to skip its `injectPrelude` pass. The user program is then
// responsible for explicitly `import`-ing every stdlib module
// it uses (`std/i32`, `std/string`, `std/array`, …).
//
// Use cases for opting out:
//   - Test programs that want to verify a single module's
//     surface works in isolation, without the rest of the
//     prelude masking missing dispatch.
//   - Programs that want a small binary footprint and don't
//     need the stdlib helpers (the IR's tree-shake usually
//     handles dead code, but skipping injection saves parse
//     + check time).
//   - Phase 5 of docs/PRELUDE-TO-MODULES.md: once every test
//     program declares its imports explicitly, the auto-
//     prelude path goes away entirely and this module's
//     presence becomes the default.
//
// The module body is intentionally empty — the import itself
// is the signal.
